<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toprakkale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · Also known as Rusahinili</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toprakkale is the site of a Urartian citadel on a hill east of Van in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, identified with Rusahinili, founded in the seventh century BCE as a new royal centre near the old capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushpa">Tushpa</a>. It contained a major temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldi">Haldi</a>. Excavations and unauthorised digging in the late nineteenth century scattered its bronze furniture fittings, shields and other finds among museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
